  "final_answer": "I now have all the information I need to provide a comprehensive answer. Let me compile the findings:\n\n**Top 3 Super-Earth Planets (Most Notable Recent Discoveries):**\n\n## 1. **LHS 1140 b** (Most Recent Breakthrough - July 2026)\n- **Distance:** ~41 light-years away (constellation Cetus)\n- **Size:** 5.6 times Earth's mass; 1.7 times Earth's radius\n- **Host Star:** Orbits an older red dwarf star (LHS 1140)\n- **Orbital Period:** 24.7 days\n- **Significance:** For the first time, astronomers detected an atmosphere (helium escaping from its upper atmosphere) on a rocky habitable-zone exoplanet using the WINERED spectrograph on the Magellan Clay telescope at Carnegie's Las Campanas Observatory. The planet receives about 42% of the stellar radiation Earth gets, placing it in the habitable zone where liquid water could exist. Discovered originally in 2017, but this atmospheric detection represents a major milestone in the search for Earth-like worlds.\n\n## 2. **GJ 3378b** (Closest Habitable Super-Earth - July 2026)\n- **Distance:** About 25 light-years away (constellation Camelopardalis, the Giraffe)\n- **Host Star:** Orbits a faint red dwarf star\n- **Significance:** One of the closest potentially habitable rocky super-Earths ever detected. The planet receives roughly 90% of the radiation Earth gets from the Sun. Initially measured at several Earth masses (in mini-Neptune territory), revised estimates suggest it may be more Earth-like. Detected using the radial velocity method (gravitational wobble) by French astronomers using the Canada–France–Hawaii Telescope.\n\n## 3. **TOI-715 b** (NASA Discovery - January 2024)\n- **Distance:** ~137 light-years away\n- **Size:** About 1.5 times as wide as Earth (super-Earth)\n- **Host Star:** Orbits a red dwarf (smaller and cooler than our Sun)\n- **Orbital Period:** 19 days (\"year\" on this world)\n- **Significance:** Discovered by NASA's TESS (Transiting Exoplanet Survey Satellite), it orbits within the \"conservative\" habitable zone around its parent star. Discovered by an international team led by Georgina Dransfield (University of Birmingham). The same system may harbor a second, Earth-sized planet, making it a prime target for future atmospheric studies with the James Webb Space Telescope.\n\n**Common Theme:** All three are super-Earths (larger than Earth but smaller than Neptune) located within the habitable zones of their host red dwarf stars — the prime targets in the search for potentially life-supporting worlds beyond our Solar System.",
